WI-bolot. `pytest --narrow` removes from the LOCAL run set only what the
coverage index can prove irrelevant. Below the seam, so the committed
manifest still publishes the full static selection.

THREE RULES, each because of a distinct way of being wrong.

1. DROP ONLY WITH POSITIVE EVIDENCE. A file qualifies only if it has
   coverage rows AND none of its tests are `unmeasured`. This is the
   precondition WI-bolot names as the one a careless implementation
   breaks, and it is conservative at file granularity on purpose.

2. KEEP THE WHOLE FILE-TOUCH SET, NOT THE CHANGED BLOCKS. WI-bolot does
   not mention the constraint that actually decides the design: the
   local gate is `coverage report --include=<changed files>
   --fail-under=100`, which is whole-FILE. Narrowing to the tests
   attached to changed blocks leaves the rest of each changed file
   uncovered and exits 1 -- a red run every commit, worse than a slow
   one. Measured at one base: block-selected 3 files against a
   file-touch set of 13, so the naive version drops 10 files that were
   carrying the coverage.

3. REFUSE OUTRIGHT on an unknown COVERABLE SOURCE file or a changed file
   that no longer exists. New blocks and unknown TEST files are reported
   and NOT refused -- the first version of the measuring script made new
   blocks a refusal and consequently reported "narrowing refused" on
   every commit examined, because ordinary development adds blocks. The
   test that covers a developer's new code is a changed test file, and
   changed test files enter the run set through a selector narrowing
   never touches. Files outside the coverage scope (scripts/, docs) are
   likewise not refusals: nothing in-process can cover them, so the
   index's silence is structural, and their subprocess tests are kept by
   rule 1 anyway.

OFF BY DEFAULT, and unconditionally off in CI -- not even
HG_SELECT_SHADOW re-enables it. Between full runs the index can be short
a test covering part of a changed file, and the gate would report that as
a coverage regression. The CI guard sits BELOW argument parsing, because
unlike the shadow/union guards this one has a flag that turns it ON and
would otherwise be overridden by the flag it exists to override.

MEASURED, both directions, on one tree:
  un-narrowed  91 files  1,680 tests  19.3s  gate 100%
  narrowed     61 files    945 tests  13.1s  gate 100%
and the manifest still reports 91.

scripts/measure-narrowing-headroom.py prices the ceiling rather than
leaving it to intuition: only 55.3% of the suite's wall-clock is
index-speakable at all, the rest being unmeasured or unseen and kept
unconditionally. On a small change the run set is already ~20s of tests,
so the saving is seconds; the win is on large selections. That number
belongs in the repo because the shadow's headline (90 files selected, 3
picked by coverage) reads like a 30x cut and is not one.

THREE INCIDENTAL FIXES.
- rebase_to_repo / selectable_test_files / files_of move from
  selection_shadow to selection_index. They convert the INDEX's output
  into the run set's vocabulary and now have three consumers; leaving
  them in the shadow module would have made narrowing import the shadow.
- The index query was first named `tests_touching`, which pytest
  COLLECTS AS A TEST in any module that imports it -- surfacing as
  `fixture 'conn' not found` at collection, an error naming nothing
  about its cause. Renamed `touching_test_files`.
- That query first used `IN (?,?,…)` with a placeholder list built from
  len(paths), which ruff (S608) and bandit (B608) both read as
  string-built SQL. Rather than annotate it twice, the filter moved into
  Python: the table is tens of thousands of rows, scanned once per
  invocation.

The whole-file gate also caught two functions shipped without unit tests
(95% on selection_index.py) on the reasoning that coverage-select
exercises them. It does, by subprocess, which contributes no coverage.

